from pathlib import Path
Path('/app/answer.json').write_text('{"answer": "The method that processes unhandled exceptions (`handle_exception` in `./src/flask/app.py`) decides whether to re-raise or convert to HTTP 500 based on configuration state.\\n\\nDecision logic (lines 841-852):\\n1. Reads `PROPAGATE_EXCEPTIONS` from config (line 841).\\n2. If `PROPAGATE_EXCEPTIONS` is `None`, it defaults to `self.testing or self.debug` (lines 843-844).\\n3. If the resulting `propagate` is `True`, the exception is re-raised (lines 846-852).\\n4. If `propagate` is `False`, it logs the exception, creates an `InternalServerError` wrapping the original, and returns a 500 response (lines 854-862).\\n\\nRequest lifecycle context:\\n- Exceptions during request handling are caught in `full_dispatch_request()` (line 918) and passed to `handle_user_exception()` (line 919).\\n- If no user error handler is found, the exception is re-raised (line 807 in `handle_user_exception`).\\n- The exception bubbles up to `wsgi_app()` (line 1512), which catches it and calls `handle_exception()` (line 1514).\\n\\nConfiguration defaults:\\n- `PROPAGATE_EXCEPTIONS` defaults to `None` (line 182 in `default_config`).\\n- When `None`, propagation is enabled if `TESTING` is `True` or `DEBUG` is enabled.\\n- This means in testing or debug mode, exceptions propagate by default unless explicitly disabled.\\n\\nCode reference:\\n```python\\npropagate = self.config[\\"PROPAGATE_EXCEPTIONS\\"]\\n\\nif propagate is None:\\n    propagate = self.testing or self.debug\\n\\nif propagate:\\n    raise e  # Re-raise exception\\nelse:\\n    # Convert to 500 response\\n    self.log_exception(exc_info)\\n    server_error = InternalServerError(original_exception=e)\\n    # ... handle and return response\\n```\\n\\nThis design allows exceptions to propagate in testing/debug for easier debugging, while converting them to 500 responses in production unless explicitly configured otherwise."}' + '\n')
